{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Eu5XZqmobLps5JuFS8u6nd","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260514T162530\n3\ttier: median\n4\toverall_score: 3/6 Weak Reject\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-14T17:11:20.963196+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t---\n9\tdream_id: dream_20260514T162530\n10\ttier: median\n11\toverall_score: 3/6 Weak Reject\n12\ttimestamp: 2026-05-14\n13\treviewer: R2 (Novelty & Related Work)\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t# Review: R2 (Novelty & Related Work)\n17\t\n18\t**Paper:** \"What Makes Hard Negatives Work for Code Retrieval? A Systematic Study of Mining Strategies and Representation Geometry\"\n19\t\n20\t**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n21\t**Confidence: 4/5**\n22\t**Novelty: 2.5/5**\n23\t\n24\t---\n25\t\n26\t## 1. Summary\n27\t\n28\tThis paper presents a controlled empirical comparison of four hard negative mining strategies (random, BM25-proxy, embedding-distance, dataflow-disconnected) for contrastive code retrieval, evaluated on two decoder-only encoders (Qwen2.5-Coder-3B, StarCoder2-3B) across SWE-smith validation, RepoBench-R, and SWE-bench Lite. The main findings are that embedding-distance mining \"tends to help\" (+0.5--1.0pp R@1), BM25-proxy negatives \"trend toward harm\" (not statistically significant), and dataflow negatives show an encoder-dependent interaction that is presented as an exploratory observation. The paper positions itself as an analysis paper contributing systematic understanding rather than a new method.\n29\t\n30\t---\n31\t\n32\t## 2. Strengths\n33\t\n34\t- **S1: Honest and well-calibrated framing.** The paper is unusually forthcoming about the limitations of its evidence. Claims are properly hedged (\"tends to help,\" \"exploratory observation,\" \"directional support\"), the SWE-bench evaluation is explicitly labeled a sanity check rather than primary evidence, and the limitations section is thorough. This level of intellectual honesty is commendable and sets a good example.\n35\t\n36\t- **S2: Comprehensive ablation suite.** The paper covers temperature sensitivity, pool size, data scaling, hop depth, static vs. dynamic mining, weighted vs. binary negatives, and a fusion head ablation. This breadth of ablation is unusual for a paper with such modest headline results and demonstrates thorough experimental methodology.\n37\t\n38\t- **S3: Stratified analysis is the paper's strongest analytical contribution.** The decomposition by negative difficulty quartile (Q1--Q4) provides a plausible representation-geometry explanation for when mining strategies help, and the structural-availability stratification clarifies the dilution mechanism. The monotonic Q1-to-Q4 pattern (embedding delta from +0.3pp to +1.6pp) is the cleanest finding in the paper.\n39\t\n40\t- **S4: Directional consistency across conditions.** Embedding-distance mining is directionally positive across both encoders, both benchmarks, all three temperatures, and all pool sizes tested. The direction never reverses for Qwen. This consistency has value even with small effect sizes.\n41\t\n42\t- **S5: Practical separation of BM25-proxy from true embedding-distance mining.** Even though the BM25 comparison does not reach statistical significance, the conceptual distinction between BM25-proxy and true embedding-distance negatives is a useful clarification for the field.\n43\t\n44\t---\n45\t\n46\t## 3. Weaknesses\n47\t\n48\t- **W1 (Major): Insufficient novelty for a top venue.** The paper's universal recommendation -- \"use embedding-distance mining\" -- is essentially ANCE (Xiong et al., 2021) applied to code. ANCE demonstrated the value of approximate nearest-neighbor hard negatives for dense retrieval five years ago. The code-specific instantiation does not constitute a novel methodological contribution. The dataflow strategy, which would have been the novel element, fails to provide consistent benefit (negative on StarCoder, CI includes zero on Qwen) and is appropriately downgraded to an \"exploratory observation.\" An analysis paper at a top venue needs to deliver substantial new understanding, but the core takeaway -- \"hard negatives help, harder is better\" -- is well-established in the contrastive learning literature.\n49\t\n50\t- **W2 (Major): The CoCoHaNeRe correction argument is too weak to serve as a primary contribution.** The paper hypothesizes that CoCoHaNeRe conflated BM25-proxy with true embedding-distance negatives, but the supporting evidence is inadequate: (a) BM25 vs. random is not statistically significant (p=0.12, p=0.18); (b) the magnitude gap is enormous (-1.9pp vs. -27pp reported by CoCoHaNeRe), which the paper attributes to \"different mining protocol, pool size, pipeline robustness\" -- but these are exactly the confounds that prevent a clean correction claim; (c) no specific evidence is provided that CoCoHaNeRe actually used BM25 as a proxy (no code citation, no methodology section quote, no author communication). The paper itself acknowledges the evidence is \"directional\" and \"insufficient to fully rule out alternative explanations.\" This is too weak to anchor the paper's narrative around \"correcting a misconception.\"\n51\t\n52\t- **W3 (Major): Effect sizes are at the noise floor for practical relevance.** The headline embedding advantage is +0.5--1.0pp R@1 at pool=50, shrinking to +0.5pp at pool=200 and +0.2pp at pool=500 for StarCoder. At realistic pool sizes (200--1000+), the advantage becomes indistinguishable from noise. The SWE-bench downstream evaluation shows delta < 1pp with p > 0.6. The paper acknowledges all of this honestly, but it raises the question: does this paper deliver actionable findings? The practical recommendation amounts to \"embedding mining is unlikely to hurt and might help a little,\" which is insufficient for a top venue.\n53\t\n54\t- **W4 (Major): Related work coverage is critically insufficient.** The paper cites exactly two references (Karpukhin 2020, Xiong 2021). For a paper positioning itself as a systematic analysis of hard negative mining, this is far below the expected standard. Critical missing references include:\n55\t  - **Hard negative mining literature**: RocketQA (Qu et al., 2021), TAS-B (Hofstatter et al., 2021), GPL (Wang et al., 2022), ColBERTv2 (Santhanam et al., 2022) -- all study hard negative strategies for dense retrieval.\n56\t  - **Code contrastive learning**: ContraCode (Jain et al., 2021), Contrastive Learning for Source Code (Ding et al., ACL Findings 2022) -- directly relevant to contrastive learning applied to code.\n57\t  - **Code retrieval systems**: SweRank and SWE-Fixer are mentioned by name but not cited. GraphCodeBERT (Guo et al., ICLR 2021) is mentioned but not cited. CodexGraph, LocAgent, RANGER, ARISE are all mentioned without citations.\n58\t  - **Curriculum-based mining**: Mentioned as a class of approaches but no specific reference given.\n59\t  \n60\t  A paper that makes claims about \"correcting misconceptions\" in the literature cannot have a bibliography of two entries. The Related Work section reads as a list of method names without proper academic attribution or comparative analysis.\n61\t\n62\t- **W5 (Significant): The dataflow contribution is dead weight.** Dataflow negatives are neutral on Qwen (+0.2pp overall, CI includes zero) and negative on StarCoder (-0.6pp). The structural extraction pipeline achieves only 34% coverage, and the 66% fallback to random sampling means the \"dataflow\" strategy is actually a mixture strategy. The hop-count ablation shows < 0.5pp differences between settings. The weighted-vs-binary ablation shows no significant difference. Taken together, the dataflow direction has produced a null result. Including it as a contribution (Contribution 4 in the introduction) -- even as \"exploratory\" -- risks giving the paper a unfocused character: it is neither a clean analysis paper about embedding vs. BM25 vs. random, nor a convincing demonstration of structural negatives.\n63\t\n64\t- **W6 (Significant): n=2 encoders cannot support the representation geometry explanation.** The \"representation saturation\" hypothesis (Qwen pos_sim=0.99 benefits from structural negatives; StarCoder pos_sim=0.80 does not) is inferred from exactly two data points. Any two encoders will differ in multiple dimensions (architecture details, pretraining data, tokenizer, training objective, model capacity allocation). Attributing the interaction to pos_sim saturation is post-hoc and unfalsifiable without additional encoders. The paper appropriately frames this as exploratory, but even as an observation, n=2 provides essentially no evidential weight for the proposed mechanism.\n65\t\n66\t- **W7: Only 3 seeds, not 9.** The paper uses 3 seeds per configuration, not the 9 claimed necessary for statistical power in the project's own design documents (D019). With 3 seeds and df=2, t-test critical values are 4.303, making it near-impossible to detect the observed deltas as significant. Bootstrap with n=3 yields only 27 resamples. The paper should either use more seeds or explicitly acknowledge that statistical tests are severely underpowered.\n67\t\n68\t---\n69\t\n70\t## 4. Questions for Authors\n71\t\n72\t1. Can you provide specific evidence (code, methodology description, or author communication) that CoCoHaNeRe used BM25 as a proxy for embedding-distance negatives? Without this, the \"proxy conflation\" hypothesis is speculative.\n73\t\n74\t2. What are the learned temperature values at convergence for each strategy? If the model has a learnable temperature parameter, strategy-specific temperature convergence could confound the comparison.\n75\t\n76\t3. At pool=500 on StarCoder, dataflow slightly exceeds embedding (0.197 vs. 0.196). Does this reversal hold across seeds, or is it within noise? How robust is the claim that \"embedding mining maintains its advantage\" when the advantage is 0.2pp?\n77\t\n78\t4. Why only 2 references in the bibliography? Is this a formatting artifact, or does the paper genuinely not cite the works mentioned in the Related Work section (SweRank, SWE-Fixer, GraphCodeBERT, CodexGraph, LocAgent, RANGER, ARISE, CoCoHaNeRe)?\n79\t\n80\t5. The dataflow strategy falls back to random for 66% of instances. Have you evaluated a variant that falls back to embedding-distance instead? This would isolate the structural signal more cleanly.\n81\t\n82\t6. How does pos_weight (positive weighting by dataflow distance) interact with the negative mining comparison? If the dataflow strategy uses different positive weighting than other strategies, the comparison is confounded.\n83\t\n84\t7. What is the compute cost of the CodeQL extraction pipeline relative to the training cost? Given the 34% coverage and null result, is the structural extraction justified?\n85\t\n86\t---\n87\t\n88\t## 5. Minor Issues\n89\t\n90\t- The bibliography should include all works mentioned in the text. Mentioning methods by name without citations is poor academic practice.\n91\t- Table numbering is inconsistent with the text in places (the paper refers to tables by different numbers than their captions).\n92\t- The abstract states \"multiple seeds\" -- specify the number (3) for transparency.\n93\t- \"CoCoHaNeRe\" is used throughout without a formal citation entry, making it impossible for readers to look up the reference.\n94\t- Section 5.4 (Pool Size Sensitivity) uses a checkmark/cross notation for \"ranking stable\" without defining what stability means precisely (same ordinal ranking? overlapping CIs? same top-1?).\n95\t- The \"representation geometry\" subtitle in the paper title overpromises relative to the content. The representation geometry analysis amounts to reporting pos_sim values for two encoders and stratifying by negative difficulty. This is descriptive statistics, not a geometric analysis.\n96\t\n97\t---\n98\t\n99\t## 6. Overall Assessment\n100\t\n101\t**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n102\t\n103\tThis paper asks a reasonable question and executes its experiments with admirable thoroughness and honesty. The ablation suite is extensive, the claims are properly hedged, and the limitations are transparently reported. I appreciate the intellectual integrity.\n104\t\n105\tHowever, the paper does not meet the bar for a top venue for several compounding reasons:\n106\t\n107\t**Novelty is insufficient.** The universal finding (\"use embedding-distance mining\") recapitulates ANCE (2021) for code. The dataflow contribution, which would have provided novelty, is a null result. The CoCoHaNeRe correction, which would have provided significance, is statistically unsupported and missing the evidentiary foundation (no proof CoCoHaNeRe actually used BM25 proxy).\n108\t\n109\t**Effect sizes are too small for practical impact.** At realistic pool sizes (200+), the embedding advantage is +0.2--0.7pp, which is within implementation noise. The SWE-bench downstream evaluation shows no significant effect. A practitioner reading this paper would conclude: \"negative mining strategy probably doesn't matter much for code retrieval,\" which is useful to know but thin as a publication contribution.\n110\t\n111\t**Related work coverage is unacceptably sparse.** Two citations for a paper claiming to be a \"systematic study\" is a serious deficiency that undermines the paper's scholarly positioning.\n112\t\n113\t**The \"analysis paper\" framing is correct but demands more insight.** Analysis papers can be valuable at top venues when they overturn conventional wisdom, reveal hidden structure, or provide actionable guidelines that change practice. This paper's insights -- embedding mining helps a little, BM25 might hurt a little, structural negatives are inconsistent -- do not rise to that level. The stratified analysis and difficulty-quartile decomposition are the strongest analytical elements, but they support a finding (mining helps more when negatives are harder) that is already well-understood from the contrastive learning literature.\n114\t\n115\tFor this paper to reach acceptance, I would need to see: (1) concrete evidence for the CoCoHaNeRe proxy claim or an honest reframing that drops the \"correction\" narrative; (2) substantially expanded related work with proper citations; (3) either more encoders to support the interaction claim or removal of the representation geometry framing from the title; and (4) a clearer articulation of what new understanding this paper provides beyond \"ANCE works for code too.\"\n116\t\n117\t**Comparison with acceptance threshold:** At a 25% acceptance rate, this paper's combination of minimal novelty, marginal effect sizes, and inadequate scholarly apparatus places it below the bar. The execution quality is good, but execution alone cannot compensate for the contribution gap.\n118\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1a45356f-658c-4c4f-ba94-f04e8cd4eef3","uuid":"a65ed097-2a92-42b2-9943-5119145a11ec","timestamp":"2026-05-14T17:12:22.168Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/structfuse-context/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T162530/median/review_report.md","content":"---\ndream_id: dream_20260514T162530\ntier: median\noverall_score: 3/6 Weak Reject\ntimestamp: 2026-05-14T17:11:20.963196+00:00\n---\n\n---\ndream_id: dream_20260514T162530\ntier: median\noverall_score: 3/6 Weak Reject\ntimestamp: 2026-05-14\nreviewer: R2 (Novelty & Related Work)\n---\n\n# Review: R2 (Novelty & Related Work)\n\n**Paper:** \"What Makes Hard Negatives Work for Code Retrieval? A Systematic Study of Mining Strategies and Representation Geometry\"\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n**Confidence: 4/5**\n**Novelty: 2.5/5**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nThis paper presents a controlled empirical comparison of four hard negative mining strategies (random, BM25-proxy, embedding-distance, dataflow-disconnected) for contrastive code retrieval, evaluated on two decoder-only encoders (Qwen2.5-Coder-3B, StarCoder2-3B) across SWE-smith validation, RepoBench-R, and SWE-bench Lite. The main findings are that embedding-distance mining \"tends to help\" (+0.5--1.0pp R@1), BM25-proxy negatives \"trend toward harm\" (not statistically significant), and dataflow negatives show an encoder-dependent interaction that is presented as an exploratory observation. The paper positions itself as an analysis paper contributing systematic understanding rather than a new method.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Strengths\n\n- **S1: Honest and well-calibrated framing.** The paper is unusually forthcoming about the limitations of its evidence. Claims are properly hedged (\"tends to help,\" \"exploratory observation,\" \"directional support\"), the SWE-bench evaluation is explicitly labeled a sanity check rather than primary evidence, and the limitations section is thorough. This level of intellectual honesty is commendable and sets a good example.\n\n- **S2: Comprehensive ablation suite.** The paper covers temperature sensitivity, pool size, data scaling, hop depth, static vs. dynamic mining, weighted vs. binary negatives, and a fusion head ablation. This breadth of ablation is unusual for a paper with such modest headline results and demonstrates thorough experimental methodology.\n\n- **S3: Stratified analysis is the paper's strongest analytical contribution.** The decomposition by negative difficulty quartile (Q1--Q4) provides a plausible representation-geometry explanation for when mining strategies help, and the structural-availability stratification clarifies the dilution mechanism. The monotonic Q1-to-Q4 pattern (embedding delta from +0.3pp to +1.6pp) is the cleanest finding in the paper.\n\n- **S4: Directional consistency across conditions.** Embedding-distance mining is directionally positive across both encoders, both benchmarks, all three temperatures, and all pool sizes tested. The direction never reverses for Qwen. This consistency has value even with small effect sizes.\n\n- **S5: Practical separation of BM25-proxy from true embedding-distance mining.** Even though the BM25 comparison does not reach statistical significance, the conceptual distinction between BM25-proxy and true embedding-distance negatives is a useful clarification for the field.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **W1 (Major): Insufficient novelty for a top venue.** The paper's universal recommendation -- \"use embedding-distance mining\" -- is essentially ANCE (Xiong et al., 2021) applied to code. ANCE demonstrated the value of approximate nearest-neighbor hard negatives for dense retrieval five years ago. The code-specific instantiation does not constitute a novel methodological contribution. The dataflow strategy, which would have been the novel element, fails to provide consistent benefit (negative on StarCoder, CI includes zero on Qwen) and is appropriately downgraded to an \"exploratory observation.\" An analysis paper at a top venue needs to deliver substantial new understanding, but the core takeaway -- \"hard negatives help, harder is better\" -- is well-established in the contrastive learning literature.\n\n- **W2 (Major): The CoCoHaNeRe correction argument is too weak to serve as a primary contribution.** The paper hypothesizes that CoCoHaNeRe conflated BM25-proxy with true embedding-distance negatives, but the supporting evidence is inadequate: (a) BM25 vs. random is not statistically significant (p=0.12, p=0.18); (b) the magnitude gap is enormous (-1.9pp vs. -27pp reported by CoCoHaNeRe), which the paper attributes to \"different mining protocol, pool size, pipeline robustness\" -- but these are exactly the confounds that prevent a clean correction claim; (c) no specific evidence is provided that CoCoHaNeRe actually used BM25 as a proxy (no code citation, no methodology section quote, no author communication). The paper itself acknowledges the evidence is \"directional\" and \"insufficient to fully rule out alternative explanations.\" This is too weak to anchor the paper's narrative around \"correcting a misconception.\"\n\n- **W3 (Major): Effect sizes are at the noise floor for practical relevance.** The headline embedding advantage is +0.5--1.0pp R@1 at pool=50, shrinking to +0.5pp at pool=200 and +0.2pp at pool=500 for StarCoder. At realistic pool sizes (200--1000+), the advantage becomes indistinguishable from noise. The SWE-bench downstream evaluation shows delta < 1pp with p > 0.6. The paper acknowledges all of this honestly, but it raises the question: does this paper deliver actionable findings? The practical recommendation amounts to \"embedding mining is unlikely to hurt and might help a little,\" which is insufficient for a top venue.\n\n- **W4 (Major): Related work coverage is critically insufficient.** The paper cites exactly two references (Karpukhin 2020, Xiong 2021). For a paper positioning itself as a systematic analysis of hard negative mining, this is far below the expected standard. Critical missing references include:\n  - **Hard negative mining literature**: RocketQA (Qu et al., 2021), TAS-B (Hofstatter et al., 2021), GPL (Wang et al., 2022), ColBERTv2 (Santhanam et al., 2022) -- all study hard negative strategies for dense retrieval.\n  - **Code contrastive learning**: ContraCode (Jain et al., 2021), Contrastive Learning for Source Code (Ding et al., ACL Findings 2022) -- directly relevant to contrastive learning applied to code.\n  - **Code retrieval systems**: SweRank and SWE-Fixer are mentioned by name but not cited. GraphCodeBERT (Guo et al., ICLR 2021) is mentioned but not cited. CodexGraph, LocAgent, RANGER, ARISE are all mentioned without citations.\n  - **Curriculum-based mining**: Mentioned as a class of approaches but no specific reference given.\n  \n  A paper that makes claims about \"correcting misconceptions\" in the literature cannot have a bibliography of two entries. The Related Work section reads as a list of method names without proper academic attribution or comparative analysis.\n\n- **W5 (Significant): The dataflow contribution is dead weight.** Dataflow negatives are neutral on Qwen (+0.2pp overall, CI includes zero) and negative on StarCoder (-0.6pp). The structural extraction pipeline achieves only 34% coverage, and the 66% fallback to random sampling means the \"dataflow\" strategy is actually a mixture strategy. The hop-count ablation shows < 0.5pp differences between settings. The weighted-vs-binary ablation shows no significant difference. Taken together, the dataflow direction has produced a null result. Including it as a contribution (Contribution 4 in the introduction) -- even as \"exploratory\" -- risks giving the paper a unfocused character: it is neither a clean analysis paper about embedding vs. BM25 vs. random, nor a convincing demonstration of structural negatives.\n\n- **W6 (Significant): n=2 encoders cannot support the representation geometry explanation.** The \"representation saturation\" hypothesis (Qwen pos_sim=0.99 benefits from structural negatives; StarCoder pos_sim=0.80 does not) is inferred from exactly two data points. Any two encoders will differ in multiple dimensions (architecture details, pretraining data, tokenizer, training objective, model capacity allocation). Attributing the interaction to pos_sim saturation is post-hoc and unfalsifiable without additional encoders. The paper appropriately frames this as exploratory, but even as an observation, n=2 provides essentially no evidential weight for the proposed mechanism.\n\n- **W7: Only 3 seeds, not 9.** The paper uses 3 seeds per configuration, not the 9 claimed necessary for statistical power in the project's own design documents (D019). With 3 seeds and df=2, t-test critical values are 4.303, making it near-impossible to detect the observed deltas as significant. Bootstrap with n=3 yields only 27 resamples. The paper should either use more seeds or explicitly acknowledge that statistical tests are severely underpowered.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. Can you provide specific evidence (code, methodology description, or author communication) that CoCoHaNeRe used BM25 as a proxy for embedding-distance negatives? Without this, the \"proxy conflation\" hypothesis is speculative.\n\n2. What are the learned temperature values at convergence for each strategy? If the model has a learnable temperature parameter, strategy-specific temperature convergence could confound the comparison.\n\n3. At pool=500 on StarCoder, dataflow slightly exceeds embedding (0.197 vs. 0.196). Does this reversal hold across seeds, or is it within noise? How robust is the claim that \"embedding mining maintains its advantage\" when the advantage is 0.2pp?\n\n4. Why only 2 references in the bibliography? Is this a formatting artifact, or does the paper genuinely not cite the works mentioned in the Related Work section (SweRank, SWE-Fixer, GraphCodeBERT, CodexGraph, LocAgent, RANGER, ARISE, CoCoHaNeRe)?\n\n5. The dataflow strategy falls back to random for 66% of instances. Have you evaluated a variant that falls back to embedding-distance instead? This would isolate the structural signal more cleanly.\n\n6. How does pos_weight (positive weighting by dataflow distance) interact with the negative mining comparison? If the dataflow strategy uses different positive weighting than other strategies, the comparison is confounded.\n\n7. What is the compute cost of the CodeQL extraction pipeline relative to the training cost? Given the 34% coverage and null result, is the structural extraction justified?\n\n---\n\n## 5. Minor Issues\n\n- The bibliography should include all works mentioned in the text. Mentioning methods by name without citations is poor academic practice.\n- Table numbering is inconsistent with the text in places (the paper refers to tables by different numbers than their captions).\n- The abstract states \"multiple seeds\" -- specify the number (3) for transparency.\n- \"CoCoHaNeRe\" is used throughout without a formal citation entry, making it impossible for readers to look up the reference.\n- Section 5.4 (Pool Size Sensitivity) uses a checkmark/cross notation for \"ranking stable\" without defining what stability means precisely (same ordinal ranking? overlapping CIs? same top-1?).\n- The \"representation geometry\" subtitle in the paper title overpromises relative to the content. The representation geometry analysis amounts to reporting pos_sim values for two encoders and stratifying by negative difficulty. This is descriptive statistics, not a geometric analysis.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n\nThis paper asks a reasonable question and executes its experiments with admirable thoroughness and honesty. The ablation suite is extensive, the claims are properly hedged, and the limitations are transparently reported. I appreciate the intellectual integrity.\n\nHowever, the paper does not meet the bar for a top venue for several compounding reasons:\n\n**Novelty is insufficient.** The universal finding (\"use embedding-distance mining\") recapitulates ANCE (2021) for code. The dataflow contribution, which would have provided novelty, is a null result. The CoCoHaNeRe correction, which would have provided significance, is statistically unsupported and missing the evidentiary foundation (no proof CoCoHaNeRe actually used BM25 proxy).\n\n**Effect sizes are too small for practical impact.** At realistic pool sizes (200+), the embedding advantage is +0.2--0.7pp, which is within implementation noise. The SWE-bench downstream evaluation shows no significant effect. A practitioner reading this paper would conclude: \"negative mining strategy probably doesn't matter much for code retrieval,\" which is useful to know but thin as a publication contribution.\n\n**Related work coverage is unacceptably sparse.** Two citations for a paper claiming to be a \"systematic study\" is a serious deficiency that undermines the paper's scholarly positioning.\n\n**The \"analysis paper\" framing is correct but demands more insight.** Analysis papers can be valuable at top venues when they overturn conventional wisdom, reveal hidden structure, or provide actionable guidelines that change practice. This paper's insights -- embedding mining helps a little, BM25 might hurt a little, structural negatives are inconsistent -- do not rise to that level. The stratified analysis and difficulty-quartile decomposition are the strongest analytical elements, but they support a finding (mining helps more when negatives are harder) that is already well-understood from the contrastive learning literature.\n\nFor this paper to reach acceptance, I would need to see: (1) concrete evidence for the CoCoHaNeRe proxy claim or an honest reframing that drops the \"correction\" narrative; (2) substantially expanded related work with proper citations; (3) either more encoders to support the interaction claim or removal of the representation geometry framing from the title; and (4) a clearer articulation of what new understanding this paper provides beyond \"ANCE works for code too.\"\n\n**Comparison with acceptance threshold:** At a 25% acceptance rate, this paper's combination of minimal novelty, marginal effect sizes, and inadequate scholarly apparatus places it below the bar. The execution quality is good, but execution alone cannot compensate for the contribution gap.\n","numLines":118,"startLine":1,"totalLines":118}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CgrStL81MgdiKVeakdc4JZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have both reviews. Let me synthesize them into a roundtable discussion and final consolidated report.\n\nR1: 4/6 (Weak Accept) — appreciates the statistical rigor, comprehensive ablations, and honest framing. Main concerns: n=2 encoders, dataflow as mixture strategy, underpowered SWE-bench, small effect sizes potentially explained by implementation details, checkpoint selection.\n\nR2: 3/6 (Weak Reject) — appreciates honesty and ablation breadth but finds insufficient novelty (ANCE for code), weak CoCoHaNeRe correction, effect sizes at noise floor, critically insufficient related work (only 2 citations), dataflow as dead weight.\n \nBoth reviewers converge on appreciating the honest framing and comprehensive ablations, while flagging the n=2 encoder limitation, the null contribution of dataflow, and the small effect sizes. Where they diverge is on whether the statistical rigor and CoCoHaNeRe correction salvage the work—R1 sees these as meaningful contributions, while R2 questions whether CoCoHaNeRe was actually applied to BM25 and remains skeptical of the overall novelty and related work coverage.\n\nFor the consolidated assessment, I'm weighing whether the bibliography gap and limited novelty can be overcome by the methodological strengths. At a top venue with this acceptance rate, the tiny effect sizes and incremental nature of the contribution make it hard to justify acceptance, even with R1's appreciation for rigor—I'm settling on a weak reject at 3/6.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4053,"cache_read_input_tokens":62109,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4053},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics